#{[17592186221952 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*"] [17592186221953 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?"] [17592186222007 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3y(t)-{y}^{2}(t)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[0,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?"] [17592186222024 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2}x)\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [0,2) \\\\ -1, & x\\in [-2,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{3}.$\n\n${a}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}"] [17592186222026 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & x\\in [0,1) \\\\ x, & x\\in [-1,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-1,1).$ \nSelect the correct options below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">I.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-1}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">II.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-1,1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">III.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\lim_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}F(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">IV.</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">{{field-4}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186229657 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*"] [17592186229664 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186234005 "For the piecewise function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+b\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+cx+2\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nit is known that $\\lim_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.$ What is the value of the constants $b$ and $c?$"] [17592186234014 "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. For what value of $k$ is $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{x-1}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2x-k, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186234352 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-2,-1]$\n\nII. $[-1,1]$\n\nIII. $(0,1]$"] [17592186240801 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240803 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 5, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{18}{{x}^{2}+2}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186240809 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247173 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247174 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247175 "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247177 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le y\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247189 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x<2y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247193 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c(1-xy),\\, & {y}^{2}\\le x\\le y, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247194 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247195 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cx{e}^{y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186247209 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<Y).$"] [17592186247211 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places."] [17592186247212 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & 0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<2).$"] [17592186247213 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y>1).$"] [17592186247214 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>1).$"] [17592186262576 "Compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262579 "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265765 "![](images/b9/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186265804 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{1+{x}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,1<y<2, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<2,\\,2<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(1,2)\\times (1,4)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186265807 "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 2x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186265808 "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+1},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-2<y<0, \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{x},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<4,\\,-2<y<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186266042 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186272284 "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?"] [17592186272285 "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?"] [17592186275459 "Compute $P(X\\le 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275460 "Compute $P(X>-3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2},\\, & -6\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278186 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k(x+1),\\, & 2<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278187 "Compute $P(0<X\\le 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({x}^{3}+1),\\, & -1\\le x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{24}x & 1\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278191 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278307 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}},\\, & x\\ge -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186278310 "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{3x+2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297242 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-9, & x<5 \\\\ \\frac{x}{5}-5, & x\\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297244 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-9, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-1, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317256 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ -2, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186317343 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | $\\lim_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |"] [17592186317392 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$"] [17592186317393 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2}, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 1, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-2.$"] [17592186317394 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186317395 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$"] [17592186317396 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"] [17592186317397 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4, & x\\in [-5,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$"] [17592186317401 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [17592186317402 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -5, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$"] [17592186323846 "Find the limit at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+1\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -4{log}_{2}⁡x\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186323847 "Find the limit at $x=3$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\pi )\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186329826 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+1, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 3, & x=1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186332845 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ x+2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186338891 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186341990 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ -1, & x<0.\\end{aligne